  "text": "To solve this problem, we need to understand the movement of the incenter \\(I\\) of triangle \\(ABC\\) as \\(C\\) moves around the semicircle.\n\n**Step 1: Understand the Geometry**\n\n- Diameter \\(AB = 18\\), so radius \\(r = 9\\).\n- Point \\(C\\) moves around the semicircle.\n- The incenter \\(I\\) of triangle \\(ABC\\) is the point where the angle bisectors meet.\n- As \\(C\\) moves, the incenter \\(I\\) traces out a locus.\n\n**Step 2: Determine the Locus of the Incenter**\n\nWhen \\(C\\) is at the endpoints of the diameter \\(AB\\) (i.e., \\(C\\) is at \\(A\\) or \\(B\\)), the triangle degenerates into a line segment, and the incenter moves to the midpoint of \\(AB\\) (i.e., \\(M\\)). However, we need to trace the movement of \\(I\\) as \\(C\\) moves around the semicircle.\n\nAs \\(C\\) moves from \\(A\\) to \\(B\\), the incenter \\(I\\) traces out a semicircle with radius equal to the distance from \\(I\\) to \\(AB\\). Since the incenter is always inside the triangle, its distance from \\(AB\\) is constant and equal to the inradius \\(r = 9\\).\n\n**Step 3: Calculate the Perimeter of the Region Bounded by the Curve**\n\nThe region traced by the incenter \\(I\\) as \\(C\\) moves around the semicircle is a semicircle with radius \\(9\\). The perimeter of a semicircle is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Perimeter} = \\pi r + 2r = \\pi \\times 9 + 2 \\times 9 = 9\\pi + 18\n\\]\n\n**Step 4: Approximate the Perimeter to the Nearest Integer**\n\nCalculate \\(9\\pi + 18\\):\n\n- \\(\\pi \\approx 3.1416\\)\n- \\(9\\pi \\approx 28.2744\\)\n- \\(9\\pi + 18 \\approx 28.2744 + 18 = 46.2744\\)\n\nRounding \\(46.2744\\) to the nearest integer gives \\(46\\).\n\n**Final Answer:**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{46}\n\\]",
